{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc \"cat /home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/AGENTS.md; cat /home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/AGENTS.md; sed -n '1,90p' '/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Rafa/20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/ETF Market Mechanics.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# Shared Vault — Codex Instructions\n\nBefore working in this vault, read [`CLAUDE.md`](CLAUDE.md) in full and follow its shared vault instructions. It is the canonical source for family context, note organization, memory policy, task management, topic preferences, git and sync precautions, and Raka operations. Do not maintain a second copy of those rules here.\n\n## Applying the shared instructions in Codex\n\n- Apply the shared rules to Codex as well as Claude. References to Claude-specific tools, permissions, memory, and skill discovery describe that runtime; use the tools actually available in this session and follow the session's system and developer instructions.\n- Read [`Rafa/AGENTS.md`](Rafa/AGENTS.md) before working in the `Rafa/` subtree. It adds the personal knowledge-work and source-processing rules.\n- Load relevant vault topic skills directly from `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/skills/<topic>/SKILL.md` and their referenced preference notes. Do not assume Claude's `.claude/skills/` symlinks make those skills available automatically in Codex.\n- Keep shared rules and corrections in the locations specified by `CLAUDE.md` and the memory policy. Keep this file limited to Codex-specific adaptations; do not copy shared preferences into machine-local agent memory.\n- Raka's Telegram integration and scheduled daily checks describe the existing bot. They do not make an interactive Codex session a scheduled check or authorize unsolicited messages. Perform that work only when requested, using available tools and the session's authorization rules. Do not create duplicate schedules.\n- When using T3 Code's browser, follow the session's collaborative preview instructions and use its `preview_*` tools when available. Report missing capabilities accurately rather than assuming another runtime's tools or permissions are available.\n\nChanges to shared vault behavior belong in `CLAUDE.md` or the relevant topic preference note so both agents use the same instructions. Changes specific to Codex belong here.\n# Agent Instructions For Rafa\n\nThis subtree is Rafa's personal knowledge-work area inside the larger shared vault.\n\n## Folder Zones\n\n- `00 Inbox/` is raw capture. Mobile notes, links, social posts, half-thoughts, and unprocessed material belong here.\n- `00 Inbox/Attention inbox.md` is for items that need Rafa's attention, judgment, or a follow-up conversation with an agent.\n- `10 Agent Work/` is the messy processing area. Use it for source expansion, draft synthesis, run logs, temporary summaries, and workflow notes.\n- `20 Knowledge/` is curated knowledge. Notes here should be durable, concise, linked, and written in Rafa's words.\n- `30 Diary/` is historical work record. Use it for daily notes, debugging/setup logs, tedious lessons learned, and \"what I went through\" records.\n- `90 Rejected/` is for reviewed material that should leave the inbox but should not become curated knowledge.\n\n## Operating Rules\n\n- Do not treat inbox items as knowledge. Process them first.\n- Do not dump raw agent output into `20 Knowledge/`. Promote only distilled ideas.\n- **Explain the thing:** describe what it is and does. Avoid “X, not Y,” “this isn’t…” and “unlike…”; move the sentence to the topic it explains, or cut it.\n  Keep a contrast only when the distinction itself is the fact being taught, when it clears up a point people commonly confuse (e.g. an ETF distributor's job is mostly legal and operational, not sales), or when it adds useful color (Rafa, 2026-11-06).\n- Use `10 Agent Work/` when extraction, comparison, or draft synthesis would create noise.\n- Slow-review files go in `10 Agent Work/reviews/` (e.g. `reviews/ETF.md.review.md`), never next to a `20 Knowledge/` note, which holds only processed knowledge.\n- Use `30 Diary/` for chronological records and hard-won operational lessons, even if they are not evergreen.\n- Preserve source URLs when promoting material into `20 Knowledge/`.\n- Prefer updating existing topic notes over creating many small notes.\n- When a source is not durable, move or summarize it under `90 Rejected/` if an audit trail is useful.\n\n## Promotion Rule\n\nPromote a source only if it changes how Rafa would think, decide, build, or explain something later.\n\n## Processing an inbox\n\n- Process an inbox when Rafa asks (approved 2026-11-05; no separate skill).\n- Apply the Folder Zones, Operating Rules and Promotion Rule above: promote into the right `20 Knowledge/` note (source URL, Rafa's words, [[wikilinks]]), file in `30 Diary/`, or move to `90 Rejected/` with a one-line reason.\n- Leave an item under **Needs Rafa** only when it needs his judgment; remove processed items from the inbox.\n- Write no digest, ingest or run-log notes for inbox processing.\n- Commit and push knowledge-bank work in small, well-described batches as you go; git history records what happened when.\n- This is Rafa's standing permission for knowledge-bank commits and pushes only, a narrow exception to vault-root `CLAUDE.md`'s no-push rule; all other work still follows that rule.\n- Run `git status` first, then `git commit -- <paths>`. Never use `-a`; follow `CLAUDE.md`'s deletion sign-off rule, and never touch `.obsidian/`.\n- **Approval:** `20 Knowledge/Finance/ETF/` holds Rafa's vouched professional knowledge and feeds etfbench. Nothing is promoted there without his approval through [[ETF#How this bank gets built|the ETF hub's review workflow]].\n- Every other topic can be promoted directly; Rafa reviews it through git history.\n- Personal to-dos only Rafa can do (e.g. measuring trousers) go in Rafa's section of `Shared/Tasks.md`, following `CLAUDE.md`'s task rules. Otherwise leave `Shared/` and raka's areas alone.\n- Ask Rafa at most three questions per batch, each with a recommended default.\n\n## Attention Escalation\n\nWhen processing sources, add a short note to `00 Inbox/Attention inbox.md` if something seems novel, surprising, or likely to change Rafa's workflow, tools, privacy posture, note structure, or future agent instructions.\n\nDo not escalate every interesting item. Use this for things that need Rafa's judgment or may change future behavior.\n\n## Research Intake\n\nRafa may run deep research outside this Codex session and drop the exported report, notes, or source bundle into `10 Agent Work/research/`.\n\nTreat those research files as intermediate material, not curated knowledge. When asked to process them:\n\n- Read the research output and preserve source links.\n- Extract durable ideas in Rafa's words.\n- Promote reusable concepts into `20 Knowledge/`.\n- Move historical or \"what happened\" material into `30 Diary/` if relevant.\n- Add a short note to `00 Inbox/Attention inbox.md` when the research suggests a possible change to Rafa's workflow, tools, privacy posture, note structure, or agent instructions.\n- Use `90 Rejected/` only when a reviewed research item should leave active attention but should not be promoted.\n\n## Twitter/X Capture\n\n- Raw Twitter/X links belong in `00 Inbox/AI inbox.md`.\n- X bookmarks arrive automatically in `00 Inbox/Social bookmarks.md` (daily timer). Route each entry to the relevant inbox or knowledge note, then delete it there.\n- Use the `x-api` MCP tools when available to read tweets by URL or ID.\n- Use `10 Agent Work/Twitter X Knowledge Capture.md` as the workflow guide.\n- Durable AI ideas should usually land under `20 Knowledge/AI/`.\n- Keep the original tweet URL as a source.\n# ETF Market Mechanics\n\nHow ETF shares come into existence, how they trade, and what keeps the price tied to the portfolio: what the industry calls ETF capital markets. Part of the [[ETF]] knowledge bank. Who bears the costs of each step is in [[ETF Participants]].\n\n## Primary vs secondary market\n\nAn ETF trades in two markets at once:\n\n- **Primary market:** authorized participants (APs) trade with the fund itself, in large blocks, by exchanging baskets of securities for ETF shares.\n- **Secondary market:** ETF investors buy and sell existing ETF shares with each other on an exchange. The fund isn't involved.\n\nMost ETF trading happens in the secondary market. The primary market is where shares are created and destroyed, and where the fund's portfolio actually changes hands.\n\n## Creation and redemption\n\n- **Creation:** an AP delivers a basket of securities to the fund and receives new ETF shares.\n- **Redemption:** an AP returns ETF shares to the fund and receives a basket of securities.\n\nThis is what keeps the ETF's market price in line with its NAV. When the price drifts away from NAV, APs profit by creating or redeeming, and that arbitrage pulls the price back. APs often act on behalf of market makers.\n\nMutual funds generally issue and redeem shares for cash, although they can redeem in kind too. Cash redemptions can require the manager to sell securities. That difference is the root of the ETF's tax advantage ([[ETF Tax Efficiency]]).\n\n## Baskets\n\n- **Pro-rata basket:** a proportional slice of the whole portfolio. This is the default for full-replication index ETFs.\n- **Custom basket:** a non-pro-rata, hand-picked set of securities. It lets the fund decide exactly which positions enter or leave, which is what makes custom rebalances and heartbeat trades possible ([[ETF Tax Efficiency#Custom rebalances and heartbeat trades]]).\n\nCustom baskets are available to index and active ETFs alike.\n\nSponsors often waive the fund's creation/redemption fee for the AP on custom redemptions, so the sponsor, not the AP, absorbs that cost (Rafael).\n\n## Basket controls and publication\n\nUS ('40 Act), ETFs relying on Rule 6c-11:\n\n- The ETF adopts written basket-construction and acceptance policies. Custom baskets need detailed parameters protecting shareholders, a process for departures, and identified adviser employee roles to review compliance.\n- Basket records include the holdings, custom-basket status and compliance, cash balancing amount and AP identity. Keep them for at least five years, the first two readily accessible.\n- The website disclosure is the portfolio, not a requirement to publish the transaction basket. These are different datasets.\n- Foreign investments delayed by local holidays or extended delivery cycles must be delivered as soon as practicable, within 15 days of redemption; this is a narrow exception to the usual seven-day limit.\n\n[Rule 6c-11(c)–(d) and (b)(4)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/17/270.6c-11).\n\nThe **PCF** is the basket file distributed through NSCC. DTCC's workflow has the ETF agent submit it on T−1 for the next trade date, with a supplemental process for amendments on T. Publication and physical file preparation follow the servicing arrangements; a bank's multiple roles do not establish which team builds the file. [DTCC timeline](https://dtcclearning.com/products-and-services/equities-clearing/etf-processing/etf-timeline.html).\n\nUS ('40 Act), ETF: the AP pays the fund's creation and redemption fees unless waived or paid by another party. These cover the fund's processing and trading costs, protecting remaining shareholders from dilution. Fixed charges may go to the custodian; variable charges go to the fund. An AP may charge its own clients a fee or spread for handling creations and redemptions on their behalf.\n\n- **Transaction fees — US ('40 Act), ETF:** “I believe” the typical maximum variable creation/redemption fee is 3% (Rafael, 2026-11-06 (practitioner knowledge; may not be public)). Corgi's filed SAI specifies “up to 3%”; that is a filing example, not a universal cap. [Corgi ETF Trust I SAI, “Creation Transaction Fees” / “Redemption Transaction Fees”](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2078265/000182912625010217/corgietftrusti_n-1aa.htm).\n\n## What can move in kind\n\n- **Basket settlement — US ('40 Act), ETF:** stocks and bonds can be delivered in kind. Derivatives in creations and redemptions are cash, though people are developing workarounds. One anecdotal example: when the AP and the swap counterparty are the same broker, a swap may be “redeemed in kind” (Rafael, 2026-11-06 (practitioner knowledge; may not be public); anecdotal). That example does not establish that derivatives are generally transferable in kind. A fund using cash settlement loses much of the in-kind benefit.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Dave Mazza, thread on ETF tax efficiency and custom baskets (2023-05-22): https://x.com/MazzaDave/status/1660793487447715841\n- Rafael (practitioner knowledge, 2026-10-25): sponsors waiving creation/redemption fees on custom redemptions.\n- Rafael, 2026-11-06 (practitioner knowledge; may not be public): cash settlement of derivatives and the anecdotal swap workaround; “I believe” a typical maximum variable fee of 3%.\n- Corgi ETF Trust I, filed SAI (2025-12-22), variable creation/redemption fees up to 3%, fixed fees to the custodian and variable fees to the fund: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2078265/000182912625010217/corgietftrusti_n-1aa.htm\n\n- Rafael (practitioner knowledge, 2026-11-06; approved by Rafa): creation/redemption fee wording and resolution of the fee conflict.\n- SEC Rule 6c-11 adopting release, transaction fees covering processing and brokerage costs to prevent dilution (p. 12 n. 27; pp. 57–58): https://www.sec.gov/files/rules/final/2019/33-10695.pdf\n- Putnam ETF Trust prospectus (2025), creation/redemption fees offsetting transfer and transaction costs: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1845809/000119312525187238/d842776d485bpos.htm\n- Davis SAI supplement (2024-07-01), fixed processing fees and variable cash fees: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1670310/000007170124000048/497.htm\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
