{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/CLAUDE.md'","aggregated_output":"# Shared Vault — Context\n\nThis is a shared Obsidian vault at `/home/rafa/Documents/zmoot`. It contains family planning, shared notes, task tracking, and other collaborative content.\n\n## User context\n- Family of four: father (Rafa), mother (Katy), two boys — Rafi (13) and Isaac (11)\n- Birthdays: **Rafa Oct 15**, **Katy Oct 19**, Rafi and Isaac both **Jul 28**. (The Oct 15 entry on Katy's calendar is Rafa's, not Rafi's.)\n- Pets: Scotty (dog) and Archer (cat)\n- Rafa's local timezone is **US Eastern (ET)**. Use ET for all time references unless otherwise specified.\n- **Rafa's terminal editor is nvim** (installed on raka-bot and the Minisforum too). Use `nvim` in any command you give him, never `nano`.\n\n### Summer traditions → the `summer` skill\nThree fixtures shape every summer: **NJY sleepaway camp (July)**, **the Ocean City week (first week of August)** and **the Labor Day camping trip**. The details, the NJY billing rules and where season notes live (`Shared/` → `zArchive/`, next-year lessons in `z2027/`) are in the **`summer` skill** → `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/preferences/Summer.md`. Load it whenever any of them comes up.\n- **⏰ Always, even if nobody mentions camp: in mid-August, raise the NJY Super Early Bird decision** (deadline early September, ~$1,000, same weekend as the camping trip).\n\n## Where knowledge goes — memory policy (Rafa, 9/26/2026)\nBefore saving anything you've learned, put it in the **narrowest place that fits**. The reasoning and examples are in `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/memory-policy.md`.\n\n| Place | Holds | Loaded | Use it? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **This `CLAUDE.md`** | Rules that apply to nearly every session | Always | ✅ Keep it lean; anything topic- or season-specific goes in a skill |\n| **Topic skills** → `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/preferences/<Topic>.md` | Rules for one topic (see *Topic skills* below) | When the topic comes up | ✅ The default for new rules and corrections |\n| **Ordinary vault notes** (trip/hub/project notes, `Shared/Tasks.md`) | Facts, history, current state | When opened | ✅ Anything that isn't a rule |\n| **Claude Code memory** (`~/.claude/projects/…/memory/`) | Facts true only on one machine | Always, on that machine | ⚠️ Almost never. Machine-only and invisible, and Raka can't write it |\n| **Per-machine `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`** | Rafa's rules for *all* his projects on one machine | Always, every project there | ⚠️ Never for Raka; only on Rafa's own machines, for rules that must follow him outside this vault |\n\n- **Tests:** a fact about a specific thing → its note. A rule for one topic → that topic's preferences note. A rule Raka would get wrong in most sessions without it → CLAUDE.md. True only on this machine → see the policy note first.\n- **When Rafa or Katy correct you,** save the rule this way and tell them where it went. **Don't write Claude Code memories.**\n\n## Vault structure\n- `Shared/` — Shared notes between family members\n  - `Tasks.md` — **Golden source** for the family task list. Read/write this directly for all task operations.\n  - `FYI.md` — Informational items from the daily checks that aren't tasks (moved out of Tasks.md 9/26/2026).\n  - `Links.md` — Reference URLs and contacts (sports teams, coaches, services, medical/dental practices)\n  - `Recipes/Weekly Purchase List.md` — running list of one-off food & household things to buy. When either parent mentions something to buy for the house, it goes here, **not** in `Tasks.md`. Everything else about groceries → the **`groceries` skill**.\n  - `Recipes/` — Recipe notes plus Katy's weekly meal plans. Each recipe is its own note; the meal-plan notes index them with wikilinks.\n  - `Potential Purchases.md`, `Trips 2026.md`, `Utility People.md`, `Weekly items for discussion.md` — Other shared family notes\n- `Reference/` — **Default location for session data files** (e.g., timestamps, state files written by automated tasks). When a recurring or automated task needs to persist data between sessions, store it here unless there's a reason to put it elsewhere. (Note: `Reference/last_gmail_check` — the daily-check epoch stamp — lives here and is referenced by absolute path in permissions and the installed scripts; do not move it.)\n  - `Reference/gitignore/` — Private/untracked files that need to live in the vault but never reach git (e.g. `safe notes.md`, live credential exports like a T3 Code pairing token). The whole folder is gitignored — drop anything git-sensitive in here rather than adding one-off `.gitignore` entries.\n- `raka/` — **Source-of-truth copies of the installed helper *scripts*** (the docs moved to the knowledge base — see below). Scripts: `claude-daily-check-poke.sh`, `claude-check-watchdog.sh`, `claude-auth-watchdog.sh` (+ its `.service`/`.timer`), and the shelved headless runner `claude-daily-check.sh`.\n- `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/` — **All Raka bot *documentation*** (moved here from `raka/` so it lives in the Obsidian knowledge base): overview (`raka.md`), droplet migrate/restart runbook (`digitalocean-migration.md`), from-scratch build guide (`new-machine-setup.md`), SSH/tailnet access (`ssh-access.md`), daily-check reliability design (`daily-check-reliability-plan.md`). These docs cross-link with Obsidian `[[wikilinks]]`; they reference the scripts in `raka/` by literal path (scripts are operational artifacts, not KB notes).\n- `zArchive/` — Archived completed projects (e.g., Japan Trip)\n- `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/preferences/` and `…/skills/` — Raka's topic preferences and skill definitions (see *Topic skills* below).\n- `Rafa/` — Rafa's personal knowledge-work area. **See `Rafa/AGENTS.md` for processing rules.** Key zones:\n  - `00 Inbox/` — Raw capture (links, half-thoughts, unprocessed material)\n  - `10 Agent Work/` — Messy processing area (drafts, source expansion, research intake)\n  - `20 Knowledge/` — Curated durable knowledge (concise, linked, in Rafa's words)\n  - `30 Diary/` — Historical work records and operational lessons\n  - `90 Rejected/` — Reviewed material not promoted to knowledge\n- `Home/` — Personal notes (e.g. `Deck.md`)\n- `Reference/gitignore/safe notes.md` — Private, synced via Obsidian only (not in git)\n\n## Twitter/X knowledge capture\n- Rafa drops Twitter/X links in `Rafa/00 Inbox/AI inbox.md`.\n- When asked to process those links, use the configured `x-api` MCP server to read each tweet by URL. Use `Rafa/10 Agent Work/` for messy source expansion or draft synthesis, then create or update durable Obsidian notes under the most relevant `Rafa/20 Knowledge/AI/` topic area.\n- Preserve the original tweet URL in the note, summarize the idea in Rafa's words, and link related vault notes with Obsidian wikilinks where there is an obvious fit.\n- `x-api` is registered in Claude Code at user scope (`claude mcp get x-api`). If a session does not expose `mcp__x-api__...` tools, check that registration; a session only picks up MCP servers at startup.\n- X bookmarks are pulled daily into `Rafa/00 Inbox/Social bookmarks.md` by a timer on the cmf. Each entry already has the full text, author and link, so routing them needs no X API calls. When asked to process them, route each entry to the relevant inbox or knowledge note (AI, Linux, ...), then delete it from that note. See [[x-api]].\n\n## Git repo\n- Branch `main`, remote `origin` at `git@github.com:rafazaya/zmoot.git`\n- `.gitignore` excludes `.obsidian/` and `Reference/gitignore/` (private/untracked files, e.g. `safe notes.md`)\n\n### ⚠️ Always validate deletions with Rafa before committing\nBefore staging or committing any `D` (deleted) file — whether it came from your own edit, an Obsidian reorg on disk, or anything else — confirm with Rafa that each deletion is intentional. Don't infer intent from context (e.g. \"this looks like scratch content\" or \"this looks superseded\") and don't assume a prior conversation's approval carries forward to new deletions.\n\n**Why:** deletions are the one change in this vault that's easy to make and easy to miss reviewing — unlike edits, there's no content left to sanity-check against. A silently-committed accidental deletion (a Sync hiccup, a bad `rm`, a folder move that dropped a file) can sit unnoticed for a long time before anyone realizes something is gone.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- List out every file being deleted (path + one-line description of what it was) and get an explicit yes before committing.\n- Approval is scoped to the deletions presented at the time — a fresh batch of deletions in a later session needs its own confirmation, even if the pattern looks similar.\n- This is separate from (and in addition to) the push policy below — local commits still need deletion sign-off, not just pushes.\n- **Frame this by actual vault content, not git status letters.** Git's `D` conflates real content loss with renames and content merges (e.g. an old note's content folded into a different note, which shows the old path as deleted and the other path as modified/new). Before presenting the list, check each `D` path against the rest of the vault and classify it as genuinely gone (no trace elsewhere), merged into another note, or a pure rename/relocation — only the genuinely-gone bucket is a \"deletion\" that needs sign-off framed as content loss.\n\n### ⚠️ Never `git push` unless Rafa explicitly asks\n**Do not push to `origin` on your own initiative.** Committing locally is fine; publishing is not. Wait for an explicit instruction (\"push\", \"push it\", \"commit and push\") — and treat that instruction as covering **only** the change in front of you, not as standing permission for the rest of the session.\n\n**Why:** this vault is edited from several machines at once, and the primary writer is **raka** — an always-running agent acting as a personal assistant. Concurrent unattended pushes are how this repo gets a tangled history, and a push is hard to walk back once other machines (or raka) have fetched it. A local commit costs nothing to amend or drop; a pushed one does not.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- Make the edit, commit if asked, then *report and stop*. Say \"ready to push, say the word\" rather than pushing.\n- Stage only the paths you actually changed. This vault is usually dirty with unrelated Sync artifacts — never `git commit -a`.\n- Never push to resolve a problem you noticed on your own (a stale ref, a missing file, a wrong file mode). Surface it and let Rafa decide which machine should own the fix.\n- Never force-push or rewrite published history.\n\n### ⚠️ Two sync mechanisms — read before trusting `git status`\nThis vault syncs by **both Obsidian Sync and git**, and **Obsidian Sync only carries Markdown**. That split (see `raka/` vs `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/` above) makes `git status` misleading on any Sync-only machine:\n\n- **Tracked non-Markdown files show as ` D` permanently** — they never reach disk via Sync, but they are intact on `origin/main`. **Never commit those deletions**; that would delete the scripts from the repo. Restore with `git restore -- raka/`.\n- **Treat every ` D` line as \"not yet synced to this machine,\"** not as an intentional deletion, until Rafa confirms otherwise. Markdown that simply hasn't arrived looks identical to a deliberate removal.\n- **Sync can also deliver a *stale* copy of a tracked Markdown file**, which surfaces as an innocuous ` M` that actually reverts newer committed content. Before committing any ` M` file you did not fully author this session, diff it against `origin/main` (`git diff origin/main -- <path>`) and confirm you are not undoing someone else's work. If the local copy is stale, `git restore -- <path>` and re-apply your edit on top.\n- **Git-only changes never propagate via Sync** (file modes, `.gitignore`, anything non-`.md`). They need an explicit push from the machine that made them and a pull elsewhere.\n- **A stale local `HEAD` compounds all of this**: Sync can deliver an upstream reorganization to disk while `HEAD` is still pre-reorg, so git reports the completed move as uncommitted deletions plus untracked additions. `git reset --mixed origin/main` advances the ref without a checkout, leaving the working tree untouched — verify with a diff against `origin/main` first.\n- `Reference/last_gmail_check` is deliberately untracked (machine-local epoch stamp). Leave it.\n\n## Google Calendar events\n- **Always pass `addGoogleMeetUrl: false` when creating an event**, unless it's actually a video call. If the option is left out, Google adds a Meet link to any event with a guest (seen 9/26/2026 on the airport-travel blocks). A Meet link can't be removed through the API afterwards; Rafa has to delete it by hand.\n- **Magnolia (renovation contractor) appointments → Rafa's calendar, with all four addresses invited: his work email `rzayas@videntam.com`, Katy's Gmail `katy.smoot@gmail.com` and Katy's work email `ksmoot@collegeboard.org`** (plus Rafa's own Gmail as organizer), unless Katy already did it (Rafa, 10/24/2026; Katy added 10/26; Katy's work email added 11/1). Details in [[Home Renovation Project]].\n- **Katy's work email `ksmoot@collegeboard.org` also goes on home or family events during the workday that could affect her work** (Rafa, 11/1/2026). If you're not sure whether one qualifies, **ask in the family group chat** (-1003764542390), not a DM, so either of them can answer.\n- **Dr. Cargan (Rafi's neurologist) appointments (Rafa, 10/27/2026):** allow **1 hr for the appointment** and block **30 min of travel on each side** (e.g. a 2–3 PM appointment is blocked 1:30–3:30), on both Rafa's and Katy's calendars with `rzayas@videntam.com` included. Whenever a new one is booked, add a Tasks.md item to **arrange with Calais to pick Rafi up**, due about a week before. Leave the driver open; that gets decided closer to the day.\n\n## Telegram integration\n- The bot (@raka_zmoot_bot) is active in a family group chat (topics/forum mode enabled, chat_id: -1003764542390)\n- Always use `reply_to` with the incoming `message_id` to keep replies in the correct topic thread\n- Two users collaborate: rafazaya / Rafa (user_id: 1553510388) and Katy (user_id: 8710865548)\n- Rafa's DM chat_id for personal notifications: 1553510388\n\n## Daily checks (8 AM and 8 PM ET)\nChecks run twice daily via a **systemd timer** (`claude-check.timer`) that injects the check into the running bot session — do **not** add an in-session cron/CronCreate for this (it would double every summary). All Raka systemd units are **system-scope** (in `/etc/systemd/system/`, owned by root, running as `rafa`) — inspect them with `systemctl` / `systemctl list-timers`, **never `systemctl --user`** (that scope is empty and makes a healthy bot look broken). After a reboot, `systemctl is-active claude-bot obsidian-sync tailscaled` confirms recovery. Each check does all steps below, then sends one Telegram summary to the family group chat (chat_id -1003764542390):\n1. **Google Calendar** — list events on both rafael.zayas@gmail.com and katy.smoot@gmail.com for the next 60 days; identify family-relevant events not yet in Tasks.md\n2. **Gmail** — search rafael.zayas@gmail.com for messages after today; scan for actionable items (appointments, school comms, financial alerts, deadlines); ignore newsletters/marketing unless time-sensitive. **Load the matching topic skill before writing about what you find:** `travel` for bookings, confirmations and itinerary changes; `summer` for camp, Ocean City and camping email; `email` before drafting any reply. **Emails from rafael.zayas@gmail.com or katy.smoot@gmail.com are highest priority** — treat them as direct instructions or information to manage. If the intent of such an email is unclear, surface it as a question in the Telegram DM summary rather than guessing.\n3. **Update Tasks.md** — add new tasks, mark [x] resolved items, put new context into the linked notes (see *Tasks.md holds tasks, not memory* below) and FYI items into `Shared/FYI.md`, and **tidy the file: move every checked-off `[x]` item out of the Open section into the Completed section** (including ones a family member checked between runs) so Open only ever holds open `[ ]` tasks\n4. **Write epoch** — after each check completes, run exactly: `date +%s | tee /home/rafa/Documents/zmoot/Reference/last_gmail_check` (this exact form is pre-approved in permissions)\n\n**Sunday 8 AM ET only — the fleet report.** Rafa's Windows PCs update overnight and a babysitter on `cachyminisforum` writes `Reference/Fleet Report.md` around 6 AM. Read it with the Read tool (no Bash). Its first line is `# Fleet report YYYY-MM-DD` (ET date); lines that need Rafa start with **NEEDS RAFA**.\n- Dated today and no **NEEDS RAFA** line: say nothing.\n- Dated today with **NEEDS RAFA** lines: DM Rafa (chat_id 1553510388) those lines verbatim, prefixed \"Fleet report:\". Not the family group, and not Tasks.md — this is Rafa's tech, not a family task.\n- Missing, or not dated today: DM Rafa \"Fleet report didn't arrive this morning.\"\n\n**Every 8 AM and 8 PM ET check — Zero Wire security alerts (Rafa, 11/3/2026).** Half an hour before each check, `zero-wire-watch` on `cachyminisforum` reads Cloudflare's Zero Wire briefings and new, prefiltered CISA KEV entries, judges them against Rafa's machines and rewrites `Reference/Zero Wire.md`. Read it with the Read tool (no Bash). Each line that needs Rafa starts `**NEEDS RAFA** (security, <check>): Zero Wire: …`, where `<check>` names one check in ET, e.g. `8 PM 2026-11-03`.\n- DM Rafa (chat_id 1553510388), word for word, only the lines whose `<check>` is **this** check (the ET date and 8 AM or 8 PM). Earlier lines were already sent; never send one twice.\n- No such line, or the file is missing: say nothing. The note's *Open for the fleet* list is reference only; never relay it.\n- Not the family group, not the family summary, not Tasks.md: this is Rafa's tech.\n\n**Every 8 AM and 8 PM ET check — tailnet outage alerts, Rafa only** (approved 2026-11-05). Read `Reference/Tailnet Watch cachyminisforum.md`, `Reference/Tailnet Watch raka-bot.md` and `Reference/Tailnet Watch Sent.md` with Read (no Bash); missing files mean no events. Each NEEDS RAFA line has `(tailnet, <event-id>)`. DM Rafa (chat_id 1553510388) only lines whose event IDs are absent from the sent ledger, preserving their wording, including the one-line back-online notices. After each successful DM, append its event IDs to `Reference/Tailnet Watch Sent.md` with Edit/Write. Never mark a failed send as sent; never resend an ID already recorded. These are durable events: relay unsent older lines too, even after a missed check or bot outage. No new IDs means silence; no all-OK line. Not the family group, family summary or Tasks.md. Producer details live in `~/dotfiles/fleet/README.md` → Tailnet outage watch.\n\n**ETF quarterly rankings — Rafa only** (approved 2026-11-06). The cmf job writes `Reference/ETF Quarterly Rankings.md` over SSH and pokes this session. On that poke, read this rule, the delivered note and `Reference/ETF Quarterly Rankings Sent.md` (missing ledger means nothing sent). The title is `# ETF quarterly rankings YYYY-MM-DD`, where the date is the researched quarter end. DM Rafa (1553510388) once per quarter end: the dated ETF inbox candidates are ready for review, with the vault commit and any stated research gaps. Link [[ETF inbox]] in the vault; these are candidates, not approved ETF knowledge. After a successful DM append the quarter end to the sent ledger. Never mark a failed send sent. No family check, family group, Tasks.md or promotion to `20 Knowledge`. Producer and manual retry: `~/dotfiles/fleet/README.md` → *Quarterly ETF rankings*.\n\n**Every 8 AM and 8 PM ET check — orggy backup failures, Rafa only** (approved 2026-11-06). Read `Reference/Orggy Backup.md` and `Reference/Orggy Backup Sent.md` with Read; missing files mean no alert. Relay only a NEEDS RAFA line tagged `(orggy-backup, <failure-id>)` whose ID is absent from the sent ledger, verbatim to Rafa's DM, never the family group or Tasks.md. After a successful send, append the ID to `Reference/Orggy Backup Sent.md`; never record a failed send or resend a recorded ID. Older unresolved alerts remain actionable after missed checks. A missing alert line means silence: no all-OK or recovery DM. The note contains operational status only, never journal entries. Producer/setup/restore: `~/dotfiles/fleet/README.md` → Orggy journal backup on cmf.\n\n**Weekly Learning — Sunday morning, Rafa only** (approved 2026-11-05). After the 08:00 ET run on cmf finishes, it writes `Reference/Weekly Learning.md` over SSH and pokes this session. On that poke, read this rule and the file with Read. Its title is `# Weekly learning YYYY-MM-DD`. Relay only a file dated today in ET, once per title date. Read `Reference/Weekly Learning Sent.md` first; if that date was already sent, stay silent. DM Rafa (chat_id 1553510388) the lessons, why they matter and recall questions, preserving their wording. Convert the collapsed Answers callout to Telegram spoiler entities (or supported spoiler formatting), so answers stay hidden until tapped. After successful sending, write the title date to `Reference/Weekly Learning Sent.md` with Write. Do not send to the family group, edit Tasks.md, create notes in Rafa/, or run Gmail/Calendar checks. A missing, stale or already-sent file means silence. The delivery poke explicitly rereads CLAUDE.md so this applies in the existing bot session. Do not also relay this during the 8 AM daily check: generation can finish after that check. Producer details: `~/dotfiles/fleet/README.md` → Weekly learning on cmf.\n\n**Tooling — keep the unattended run from stalling on a permission prompt.** Do the whole check with only the allowlisted tools: Gmail/Calendar MCP reads, the Edit/Write tools for `Tasks.md` (the vault `**/*.md` glob is allowlisted — use Edit to add, check off, AND move completed items to the Completed section), the Telegram reply/react tools, and the single pre-approved `date +%s | tee …/last_gmail_check` Bash command. Do **not** shell out for anything else — no `cat`/`date` staleness pre-check, no `journalctl`/diagnostics, no Python/awk to rearrange lines. Any non-allowlisted Bash triggers a \"Do you want to proceed?\" prompt that hangs the unattended 8 AM/8 PM run (this was the cause of the missed morning checks). Likewise never send the Telegram summary via raw `curl`/bot-token — use the reply tool only.\n\nEither family member can also add/complete tasks via Telegram; the bot updates `Tasks.md` accordingly.\n\n**1 PM ET midday check (Rafa only).** A separate timer runs the jobs in `Reference/Midday Check.md`: Rafa's personal watches and one-offs, such as price watches. It messages only his DM and stays silent when there's nothing to report. When Rafa asks for a recurring personal check that Katy doesn't need to see, add it there, not to Tasks.md's Recurring Reminders.\n\n**Always present BOTH date and time in Eastern Time (ET).** The system/host clock reports in UTC, so during the 8 PM ET run the UTC date has already rolled to the next day — do **not** label the summary with that UTC date. Convert to ET first, then stamp the summary header and every date/time in it with the ET date and time (e.g. the 8 PM run on Sat Jun 27 ET must read \"Saturday 8 PM, Jun 27\", not \"Sunday … Jun 28\"). This applies to the Telegram summary header, task dates, and any date reference you write.\n\n## Topic skills — preferences load when the topic comes up (Rafa, 9/26/2026)\nRafa's preferences mostly apply to things that come up only now and then, like rental cars. So instead of loading every rule into every session, each **topic** has a skill that loads its rules only when needed. **Both the rules and the skill definitions live in the Raka folder in Obsidian:** preferences in `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/preferences/<Topic>.md`, skill definitions in `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/skills/<topic>/SKILL.md`. Each `.claude/skills/<topic>` is a **symlink** to its folder there, because Claude Code only discovers skills under `.claude/skills/`.\n\n- **✈️ Travel → the `travel` skill.** It exists to help Raka help Rafa with travel planning. **Load it before handling anything travel-related:** flights, hotels, rental cars, itineraries, booking confirmations, trip notes, packing, eSIMs or travel calendar events, including when one turns up in the daily check. The details are in `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/preferences/Travel.md`.\n- **When Rafa or Katy corrects you on a topic that has a skill, add the rule to that topic's preferences note**, not to Claude's memory. Leave the `SKILL.md` alone unless Rafa asks. It only says *when* to load the rules.\n- **Compaction: let skill rules fall away.** When summarizing the conversation, don't restate rules from preference notes; at most note which skills were in use. Afterwards, don't re-load them up front. Load a skill again only when its topic next comes up, and read its note fresh then, so edits Rafa made in Obsidian always win over an old summary. (The bot also restarts nightly; see raka.md.)\n- **Adding a topic:** create `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/skills/<topic>/SKILL.md` and `preferences/<Topic>.md`, then symlink `.claude/skills/<topic>` → `../../Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/skills/<topic>` **on each machine**. Obsidian Sync carries the `.md` files but not the symlink. All the links are tracked in git, so commit a new one once and other machines get it with `git pull`. When you make one by hand, use `ln -sn` (works on Linux and macOS), never plain `ln -s` or `ln -sf`. If the link already exists, `ln -s` follows it and quietly drops a broken link *inside* the skill folder (this happened to `flights` on 10/11).\n- **✉️ Email → the `email` skill.** Load it before drafting, replying to or forwarding any email on Rafa's behalf. Signature, cc's and tone are in `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/preferences/Email.md`.\n- **🏕️ Summer → the `summer` skill.** NJY camp, Ocean City and Labor Day camping; see *Summer traditions* above. Rules in `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/preferences/Summer.md`.\n- **🛒 Groceries → the `groceries` skill.** Load it before building, reviewing or changing an Instacart cart, making a shopping list or planning the week's food. The store (Wegmans), the lists and the ordering rules are in `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/preferences/Groceries.md`.\n- **🧹 Vault cleanup → the `vault-cleanup` skill.** Load it when Rafa asks to tidy, archive or sweep `Tasks.md` or old trip/project notes. Run on request, not on a schedule. Procedure in `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/preferences/Vault Cleanup.md`.\n- **🛫 Flights → the `flights` skill.** Look up flight status (FlightStats) and fares yourself whenever a specific flight or fare comes up. Never tell Rafa to \"check the app\" first. It's a lookup tool, so its instructions live in its `SKILL.md`; the travel rules stay in Travel.md.\n- More topics (house/contractors, money/bills, Rafi's school, Raka maintenance) will get the same treatment. Add them to this list.\n\n## Bot self-documentation — keep `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/raka.md` current\n`Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/raka.md` is the human-readable overview of how this bot works (host, systemd services, daily checks, Telegram, sync), written to stay useful **independent of any Claude session**. **Whenever the bot's architecture or operation changes** — host, systemd services, launch flags, scheduling, sync setup, Telegram access, or the daily-check steps — update `raka.md` in the same change so it never drifts. The deep setup/restart-recovery runbook is `Rafa/20 Knowledge/Projects/raka/digitalocean-migration.md`; the from-scratch build guide is `new-machine-setup.md`. (All raka docs live together in that knowledge-base folder; the installed helper scripts' source-of-truth copies stay in the vault-root `raka/` folder.)\n\n## Task management\n- `Shared/Tasks.md` is the single source of truth for family tasks\n- Tasks are **shared between Rafa and Katy** — either can own, act on, or complete any item. Don't assume a task is Rafa-only just because it came from his Gmail. When in doubt, phrase tasks so either parent can pick them up rather than assigning to a specific person.\n- Tasks come from daily checks (Gmail + Calendar), Telegram requests, or manual adds\n- Format: checkbox markdown (`- [ ]` open, `- [x]` done) with a bold title and **one short line** of detail\n- Sections: Open, Completed, Recurring Reminders. Informational items (weather advisories, \"already happened\", \"no action needed\", receipts, account balances) go in `Shared/FYI.md`, **not** Tasks.md.\n\n### ⚠️ Tasks.md holds tasks, not memory (Rafa, 9/26/2026)\nRafa was disappointed that Tasks.md had filled up with trip itineraries, confirmation numbers, lawsuit chronology and running commentary. Keep it to **what still needs doing**:\n- **One entry per action**: a bold title, the deadline, who/where to call if needed, and a `[[link]]` to the note that holds the context. At most one or two short sub-bullets.\n- **Anything complicated or multi-day/week/month gets its own note** (or goes into an existing one), and the task links to it. Examples: trips → `Shared/<year> <Place> Trip.md`; Rafi's case/Calais → `Shared/Rafi Law Firm Case.md` and `Shared/Rafi School Search.md`; renovation → `Shared/Home Renovation Project.md`; snowblower → `Reference/Snowblower Plan.md`.\n- **When new information arrives on an existing task, update the note, not the task.** Change the task line only if the action, deadline or owner changed. Don't append \"UPDATED…\", \"NEW…\", quotes from emails, or analysis to task entries.\n- **No booking details in Tasks.md.** Confirmation numbers, flights, seats, prices and cancellation windows belong in the trip note.\n- **When a date passes, move the item to Completed as a one-liner.** If you can't tell whether it was done, list it under \"❓ Past their date — confirm done, or drop\" rather than keeping the full entry.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"Rafa’s practitioner estimate, as of November 6, 2026, is **3%** as the typical maximum variable creation/redemption fee for US ’40 Act ETFs—qualified as **“I believe,”** rather than an established industry-wide rule.\n\nThe cited [Corgi ETF Trust I SAI](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2078265/000182912625010217/corgietftrusti_n-1aa.htm), under “Creation Transaction Fees” and “Redemption Transaction Fees,” specifies variable fees **“up to 3%.”**\n\n**Worth knowing:** Corgi provides a concrete filed example consistent with Rafa’s estimate. It does **not** establish a universal or regulatory 3% cap; a particular fund’s applicable filed terms govern its maximum."}}
